# Web UI

Layout, disclosure, and status rules: `architecture.mdc`.

## Never hardcode a manager decision

Any value a manager can set is passed from her input. The UI never substitutes a literal for a manager decision, and never records a literal in a calculation snapshot.

Race rule, target share, turnout rates, multipliers, capacity, productivity, costs — all come from campaign inputs. If a screen needs a value the input layer doesn't supply yet, say so and stop. Do not substitute a literal to make the screen render.

A placeholder that ships becomes the behavior. `planBuilder.ts` hardcoded `{ type: 'MAJORITY', requiredShare: 0.5 }` for ten sprints, which made the election-type field decorative and produced a false majority line on a plurality race.

## Calculation snapshots record what the formula consumed

Every input the calculation actually used, with the values used. Engine Rule 10 requires reproducibility from stored inputs. A snapshot recording `{ eligibleVoters }` for a formula that consumed three propensity counts and three turnout rates cannot be reproduced, and adopted plans are immutable — a bad snapshot cannot be corrected later.
